#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "781f5c90-7d84-554a-9131-efc9f079d16a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4203" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n5+7+9+\\cdots = 140\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big( 2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140,$ and the first term $a_1=5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5)+(N-1)(2)) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+2N-2) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N+8) \\\\ 140 & ={N}^{2}+4N \\\\ 0 & ={N}^{2}+4N-140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-140)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 24}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078221 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "ca02b0a6-5fe1-55da-9c59-844999c2a160" :question/math-academy-id "e-4204" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14,$ the $7$th term $a_{7}=19,$ and the sum of the series is $S_N=850.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big( 2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{5}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14.$ We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+4\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+10 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4,$ $d=2.5,$ and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4)+(N-1)(2.5)) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8+2.5N-2.5) \\\\ 850\\cdot 2 & =N(5.5+2.5N) \\\\ 1700 & =2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N-1\\,700 & =0 \\\\ 5{N}^{2}+11N-3\\,400 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(11)\\pm \\sqrt{(-11)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-3400)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm \\sqrt{68\\,121}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm 261}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=25$ or $N=-27.2.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=25$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078222 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "badf4823-0460-5e11-a58b-e817c80416d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4205" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is $a_1=2,$ the common difference is $d=4,$ and the sum of the series is $S_N=200.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a_1=2,$ $d=4,$ and $S_N=200$ into the above and solve for $N,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4+4N-4) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\ 200 & =2{N}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have taken the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7118e524-eade-539a-b842-cdf6cc529568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320096} {:answer/id #uuid "c9115be1-0325-5557-984b-b560bfb34baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320097} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f196ed-5304-585c-8dde-fcbf1d27d35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320098} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfa87fd-3f36-56a8-81ea-a99dabe2a429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320099} {:answer/id #uuid "51b7e4aa-43de-5055-b43f-63c355398670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9115be1-0325-5557-984b-b560bfb34baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320097} :answer-field/id #uuid "7198a3cd-1df7-576d-afcf-4c9aa9ea3289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26800328-a78b-5992-a8a5-3714ffd4aa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-108898" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1,$ the common difference is $d=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=52.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1,$ $d=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${S}_{N}=52$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 52 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =N(2+\\frac{N}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}N \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-208 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-208)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{841}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 29}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=13$ or $N=-16.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=13$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "469992d5-e3fe-5d0a-be28-ef433115f2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320103} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7390e8-5611-576f-bfcf-649aa0fc4ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320104} {:answer/id #uuid "ca97d072-9e9a-50bc-bab9-ce6ce8d58b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320105} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecca16c-ae82-5b75-974d-8056364b7126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320106} {:answer/id #uuid "61b8ca28-a0fe-593a-8070-79e186e861d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "469992d5-e3fe-5d0a-be28-ef433115f2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320103} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e3f0c6-b6ed-5913-ad69-8beca185d40c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f6251eb-1bcd-508b-8858-dbeab480b733" :question/math-academy-id "q-108900" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=5,$ the common difference is $d=-2.5,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=-105.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=5$, $d=-2.5$ and ${S}_{N}=-105$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -105 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot (-2.5)) \\\\ -210 & =N(10-2.5N+2.5) \\\\ -210 & =12.5N-2.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}-12.5N-210 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(-12.5)\\pm \\sqrt{(-12.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 2.5\\cdot (-210)}}{2\\cdot 2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5\\pm 47.5}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-7.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdda60c-e14e-510c-805a-eece1a3d5da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320110} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbd46a6-a4f6-53bc-a265-877fdd59ad1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320111} {:answer/id #uuid "13a355f0-e5d5-54ea-8af2-f750e2e043d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320112} {:answer/id #uuid "5b33ed6a-4732-59e9-88bf-7f41f178560e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320113} {:answer/id #uuid "b3274cd9-ff9a-58b7-b922-ec5fe6fba2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b33ed6a-4732-59e9-88bf-7f41f178560e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320113} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ac89c9-c720-56e0-abd4-3d1642bfe049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ce8884-497d-55e7-a6f2-e2dbf44b2682" :question/math-academy-id "q-108902" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+9+12+…=195\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=195,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 195 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(6)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 390 & =N(12+3N-3) \\\\ 390 & =N(9+3N) \\\\ 390 & =3{N}^{2}+9N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+9N-390 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-130 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-130)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 23}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-13.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b16e49-72b9-5269-b666-956b3d6ddbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320117} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5b0d93-11fe-5269-92dd-e9604b7031f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320118} {:answer/id #uuid "bc53e1e3-1220-59d7-9425-1b4dddba114c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320119} {:answer/id #uuid "8edc0318-7e6e-558a-9ea8-600152e4674a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320120} {:answer/id #uuid "d14c61ed-4439-5a3b-85f4-6ce8278ad3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc53e1e3-1220-59d7-9425-1b4dddba114c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320119} :answer-field/id #uuid "96babc3e-483b-50e6-af7e-40b15b989d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "019294fd-8991-5ecf-84c5-32be4863e970" :question/math-academy-id "q-108904" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n2+2.5+3+…=470\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=470,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =2.5-2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 470 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 940 & =N(4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 940 & =N(0.5N+3.5) \\\\ 940 & =0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N-940 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+7N-1\\,880 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{(-7)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1\\,880)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{7\\,569}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm 87}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=40$ or $N=-47.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=40$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a0f6ad4-256e-5219-9e1a-430ca09bcf5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320124} {:answer/id #uuid "7a285f3b-29f7-5c79-8add-78f6dc3b444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320125} {:answer/id #uuid "26456f80-c8b0-596a-a44b-b8661496852e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320126} {:answer/id #uuid "d63d280c-9658-5384-9965-7ebb6a371d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320127} {:answer/id #uuid "41627011-49ee-5f20-916a-e2fee389004c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26456f80-c8b0-596a-a44b-b8661496852e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320126} :answer-field/id #uuid "271d0c04-b314-5e06-9bc7-66dbebe969ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5286cf60-565c-5d5c-9d85-e272bdfe6dfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-108910" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n5.5+6+6.5+...=77.5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=77.5,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=5.5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =6-5.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 77.5 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5.5)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 155 & =N(11+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 155 & =N(10.5+0.5N) \\\\ 155 & =10.5N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+10.5N-155 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm \\sqrt{(-10.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-155)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm 20.5}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-31.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cfeeb64-956b-5a03-b3f1-9652d75af5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320131} {:answer/id #uuid "9026bb4f-91e3-5fc2-81df-e0daa5756744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320132} {:answer/id #uuid "26794ed4-f831-5a41-9c1f-4bb9d2c3ca62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133} {:answer/id #uuid "955014a5-b99d-5730-b74a-5278f7d99fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320134} {:answer/id #uuid "8c09dcd8-773f-5705-bef1-7cbcb515ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c09dcd8-773f-5705-bef1-7cbcb515ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320135} :answer-field/id #uuid "32fba71b-d306-58c0-8593-e4f7022c16a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b651561-8f3c-5dbc-aa84-6fde737a1f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-108928" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=-3,$ the second term is ${a}_{2}=0,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=27.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know the first two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=-3,$ $d=3,$ and ${S}_{N}=27$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(-3)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 54 & =N(-6+3N-3) \\\\ 54 & =N(3N-9) \\\\ 54 & =3{N}^{2}-9N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the above equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{N}^{2}-9N-54 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}-3N-18 & =0 \\\\ (N-6)(N+3) & =0 \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive solution because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186460e7-a21f-599c-9883-771880e71637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320138} {:answer/id #uuid "ca421a85-03fc-50d6-a165-44fa1ef8fc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320139} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b2f9e0-fef4-5dc9-b763-74db7e79ceef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320140} {:answer/id #uuid "9376f66c-49d5-558d-b468-fc9bfd7092b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320141} {:answer/id #uuid "e87dd654-c121-58be-9669-3582867c7c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9376f66c-49d5-558d-b468-fc9bfd7092b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320141} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f9142c-e1d3-5ad7-981a-09b4a14f1cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2bb88b4-89d4-55c4-932b-aedd9414b602" :question/math-academy-id "q-108930" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=-1$ the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=1.5,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=6.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{1}}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5-(-1)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.5}{5} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=0.5$, and ${S}_{N}=6$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot (-1)+(N-1)\\cdot 0.5) \\\\ 12 & =N(-2+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 12 & =N(0.5N-2.5) \\\\ 12 & =0.5{N}^{2}-2.5N \\\\ 24 & ={N}^{2}-5N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{2}-5N-24 & =0 \\\\ (N-8)(N+3) & =0 \\\\ N & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive solution because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c18a66-cd69-5944-96b5-215a33f8dd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145} {:answer/id #uuid "bde9b4bb-4fc9-5250-b0d3-ea472176a10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320146} {:answer/id #uuid "b694e76a-b7a5-589f-8748-8c73b02cf25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320147} {:answer/id #uuid "f9779fe2-a96d-56f7-87cf-5e349f2d8d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148} {:answer/id #uuid "c54c484f-aa7b-5f55-a1c1-eef84d9309d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9779fe2-a96d-56f7-87cf-5e349f2d8d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f12a6e3-a4e3-59ca-9ee6-67f1bd3184a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "954874f8-e04b-5bfd-8b04-4cc402d26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39361" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=-3$, the $5$th term ${a}_{5}=5$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=192.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{1}}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-(-3)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=-3$, $d=2$, and ${S}_{N}=192$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(-3)+(N-1)(2)) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(-6+2N-2) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N-8) \\\\ 192 & =N(N-4) \\\\ 192 & ={N}^{2}-4N \\\\ {N}^{2}-4N-192 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-192)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{784}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 28}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=16$ or $N=-12.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=16$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce20eb-f209-59d8-81ff-177df8dc45fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077422} {:answer/id #uuid "fdda9447-7ba4-55f0-93ed-75e96a872439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077423} {:answer/id #uuid "43af3c56-f5d5-51d0-9a7a-9e4290a8b1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077424} {:answer/id #uuid "5642cb7f-3598-570f-96ee-60cbd17492f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077425} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc0c5b1-77dd-5404-9865-b23e5162dfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fce20eb-f209-59d8-81ff-177df8dc45fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077422} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8d7cff-adad-57df-899a-ef4ec0ce90df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71cd7c6d-9bf1-5897-a501-31a80738abbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-5446" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=4$, the $8$th term ${a}_{8}=60$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=1600.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-4}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{7} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=4$, $d=8$, and ${S}_{N}=1600$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 4+(N-1)\\cdot 8) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8+8N-8) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8N) \\\\ 1600 & =4{N}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4860165-c4b1-56c7-a519-137a7054b977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320152} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff26050-e2e0-5358-b0e3-6afefed0168d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320153} {:answer/id #uuid "9d991d0d-47e9-50e2-a9a9-d7bdb505ed81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320154} {:answer/id #uuid "104ff121-4e5b-5bab-a530-6f1de8279731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320155} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc8a881-02e8-5297-9838-e9a7e5bb46fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4860165-c4b1-56c7-a519-137a7054b977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320152} :answer-field/id #uuid "87291b6e-04b4-580a-9938-35597db2c6b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9049708-7958-580a-b104-302533a46387" :question/math-academy-id "q-5486" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=9$, the common difference is $d=8$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=636.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=9$, $d=8$ and ${S}_{N}=636$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 9+(N-1)\\cdot 8) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(18+8N-8) \\\\ 636 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8N+10) \\\\ 636 & =N(4N+5) \\\\ 636 & =4{N}^{2}+5N \\\\ 4{N}^{2}+5N-636 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4\\cdot 4\\cdot (-636)}}{2\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{10\\,201}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 101}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-\\frac{106}{8}.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3e64f5a-917c-54bc-9b45-769044ef7ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320159} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaec3dd-1c86-5809-8500-c9ec4b1733ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320160} {:answer/id #uuid "11bc0ded-6851-551c-8a77-73ddf1ac8b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320161} {:answer/id #uuid "f22945e4-33ea-5ddf-a4c0-f554964e3ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320162} {:answer/id #uuid "91c90976-f8cf-5084-b0d0-31c76e9d86c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddaec3dd-1c86-5809-8500-c9ec4b1733ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320160} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60e1c3e-a487-5c04-be1d-b9793ee0daf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b22671b2-1010-5433-aba5-f9f03ff0c371" :question/math-academy-id "q-5490" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=3$, the common difference is $d=6$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=243.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=3$, $d=6$ and ${S}_{N}=243$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 3+(N-1)\\cdot 6) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6+6N-6) \\\\ 243 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6N) \\\\ 243 & =3{N}^{2} \\\\ {N}^{2} & =81 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c248368-5133-5715-b76a-0a107f1f03f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320166} {:answer/id #uuid "8983e7e6-4be0-578c-b5e0-7e07664b4d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320167} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9078c-860a-519d-9a44-789a96bf4990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320168} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7b0f83-4b48-5359-970b-41499a79f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320169} {:answer/id #uuid "70e16faf-e0ca-52a3-933c-4284a70f0704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9078c-860a-519d-9a44-789a96bf4990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320168} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69fd115-e00b-5d18-b107-611c6828fefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9526c9b9-0eed-5271-99f3-3abc0c11c0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-5491" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1.5$, the common difference is $d=3$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=150.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1.5$, the common difference is $d=3$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=150.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series.\n\nTo find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1.5$, $d=3$ and ${S}_{N}=150$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot (1.5)+(N-1)\\cdot 3) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(3+3N-3) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{N}{2}(3N) \\\\ 150 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {N}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff20f1da-c379-526b-b3ae-84ac11f270fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320173} {:answer/id #uuid "cab9cadb-055b-5a1f-bee2-16324732410f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320174} {:answer/id #uuid "e496d045-25ea-5cc7-adc4-4855e271b577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320175} {:answer/id #uuid "5adb014c-77b3-536c-8a56-0f4ec2f0011b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320176} {:answer/id #uuid "97f76d3a-f4fc-51c1-9bf7-4830113c056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5adb014c-77b3-536c-8a56-0f4ec2f0011b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320176} :answer-field/id #uuid "27903b18-02c3-50a1-b22d-f43d11e66a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2edd6131-c051-5c20-809e-c51cb954b96d" :question/math-academy-id "q-5492" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=12$, the second term is ${a}_{2}=36$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=588.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know the first two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =36-12 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=12$, $d=24$, and ${S}_{N}=588$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 588 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(12)+(N-1)(24)) \\\\ 588 & =\\frac{N}{2}(24+24N-24) \\\\ 588 & =\\frac{N}{2}(24N) \\\\ 588 & =12{N}^{2} \\\\ 49 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fc5f1d-0cdc-5d09-8213-fca1bf068d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320180} {:answer/id #uuid "1d29c172-7fcb-5c67-b9c8-97f277bea25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320181} {:answer/id #uuid "2bbffd53-746e-5e86-98b8-00acfdbfc0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320182} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa34b8b-038a-5bf6-969e-de64c4dc5360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320183} {:answer/id #uuid "33dc41ff-29fb-5cb2-b109-5d09c87279de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33dc41ff-29fb-5cb2-b109-5d09c87279de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320184} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de14f00-92a6-590e-a4ad-eedb0df3d576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "024429c3-b713-5abe-8b1b-efafd33062eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-67075" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n7+21+35+…=175\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=175$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=7.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =21-7 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 7+(N-1)\\cdot 14) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(14+14N-14) \\\\ 175 & =\\frac{N}{2}(14N) \\\\ 175 & =7{N}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=5$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb066679-3bd9-5b33-8365-2de38396c167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320187} {:answer/id #uuid "f71db5de-4fd3-573b-a3a0-4480c987d878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320188} {:answer/id #uuid "e1766094-c25c-501b-8204-e3696993bc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320189} {:answer/id #uuid "a07e28dd-4d3a-5272-b96d-4b94d64a794b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320190} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d06aa-de82-5622-8ab0-5434534c1bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d06aa-de82-5622-8ab0-5434534c1bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320191} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ec5ddf-6174-54c0-9545-03f099127179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2441aba-57e7-567c-9abb-0943ca4d4af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67077" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+18+30+…=216\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=216$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =18-6 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 6+(N-1)\\cdot 12) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(12+12N-12) \\\\ 216 & =\\frac{N}{2}(12N) \\\\ 216 & =6{N}^{2} \\\\ 36 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$can't be negative. Therefore, our series has $N=6$ terms."}]}
